KG42.register("nodes-830d29350d22112a7e955b7d",{"id":"COMP-e02ca159eb62d0e4","code":"OMC041","label":"Pareto coalition reorganization and state-dependent member selection","definition":"Includes two kinds of organizational definition: coalition merging and splitting are executed according to the source-defined Pareto order on individual payoffs; members of W/V and of provider K and receiver L are determined according to state, price thresholds, utility comparison, and adjusted transaction quantity. Trading groups are reorganized on this basis in each time slot.","layer":"L4","template_code":"CBA","concept_id":"matching.counterparties","concept_ids":["matching.counterparties"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Object: the set of prosumer coalitions and their trading-role groups","Core operation one: preference-permitted coalition merging, splitting, and migration across time slots","Core operation two: member selection driven by state and transaction conditions","Output: coalition structure and the members of each group, with process rules and member predicates retained separately"]},"exclusions":["The source Pareto condition is that every member has u_A(n)≥u_B(n), and at least one member takes equality; it is not changed to at least one person strictly improving","State 1 and state 2 are not mixed; W is initially selected when the residual electricity deficit satisfies E_{n,def}(t)>0","When supply is in surplus, the receiver enters V, and the provider selects V/W according to whether the adjusted discharge quantity is positive","The demand-surplus branch judges the received quantity, yet it is printed as Prosumer n; the participant-index ambiguity is unresolved","The L condition of Equation (20) contains p_d(t)≥p_{n,d}(t) and is retained separately from the charging branch of Table 2","When the bidirectional incentive holds, the maximum utilities of charging and discharging are compared; when they are equal, no selection rule is given"],"members":[{"id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Splitting from the previous coalition and merging with state-2 peers precedes trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Scope: Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. Condition: A prosumer in state 1 at t-1 chooses state 2 at t and changes coalitions. State-1 trading within coalition 1 uses the mid-market pricing scheme referenced in [39]. Scope: P2P trading among members of coalition 1. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 1. Trading within coalition 2 uses the local trading prices derived in (18) and (19). Scope: P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 2. The pricing design removes the possibility of a contemporaneous trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries for trading and prosumers who do. Scope: Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. Condition: Prices without battery-degradation compensation fall within case 3 of Table 2, as specified for the proposed model. State-1 trading assumes zero contribution of battery energy to flows across trading entities. Scope: Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. Condition: The prosumer is in state 1. State-2 trading permits selling energy discharged from a battery to other prosumers and buying peer energy to charge a battery for future needs. Scope: Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 2. The source constrains battery exchange so charging and discharging exceed neither the rated battery rate nor the network energy-transfer limit; the network transfer cap protects recommended node-voltage limits. Scope: State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network. Condition: Battery charging or discharging is undertaken for P2P trading at time t. Total energy charged by receivers must equal total energy discharged by providers. Scope: Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. Condition: State-2 providers and receivers exchange battery energy through P2P trading. The baseline model does not include state-2 sales of battery energy to the CPS. Scope: Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. Condition: A state-2 prosumer would sell battery energy to the CPS. In this proposed extension, a prosumer may sell energy to the CPS through the P2P mechanism. Scope: Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. Condition: The CPS participates in the local market as a peer and allows prosumers to negotiate the per-unit price of energy sold to it.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. | Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. | Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. | Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. | Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. | P2P trading among members of coalition 1. | P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. | Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. | Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. | State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24","v141-b-g01-E009","v141-b-g01-E010","v141-b-g01-E013","v141-b-g01-E014","v141-b-g02-E26","v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E34"]},"constraint_refs":["31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","source_rule_sha256":"56f4c146060c93c1fc0141a3c86155258f96faaa3a2fec56e3882d5d888fdf77","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"Local quantity allocation feeds final coalition grouping: all receivers choose type 2; providers choose type 2 when their adjusted discharge quantity is positive and W otherwise. Scope: Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-supply branch. Condition: Total provider discharge exceeds total requested receiver charging energy. Local quantity allocation feeds final coalition grouping: all providers choose type 2, while the adjusted receiver charging quantity is tested for positivity to select V rather than W membership. The source's participant-index ambiguity remains unresolved. Scope: Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-demand branch. Condition: Total requested receiver charging energy exceeds total provider discharge. Available P2P trading prices enter the prosumer's decision about state 1 versus state 2 and the corresponding trading coalition, together with individual price thresholds and Pareto comparison. Scope: Individual coalition choice in each time slot of the community P2P market. Each prosumer selects its coalition under the source-defined Pareto order with the objective of maximizing its individual utility, using merge-and-split rules. Scope: Individual coalition selection in each time slot. A collection of disjoint coalitions may agree to merge into its union coalition. Scope: Merge operation under Definition 2. Condition: The proposed union coalition is preferred by the players over the existing collection according to the source-defined Pareto order. A coalition may split into the preferred collection of smaller coalitions. Scope: Split operation under Definition 3. Condition: The resulting smaller coalitions are preferred by the players over the original coalition according to the source-defined Pareto order. A coalition cannot mix prosumers who do not charge or discharge batteries for trading with prosumers who charge or discharge batteries for trading. Scope: Composition of trading coalitions in the proposed scheme. The coalition formation algorithm runs in every time slot to reconfigure membership for maximum prosumer benefit. Scope: Time-slot coalition reconfiguration. At each time slot, each prosumer forms a coalition with state-1 or state-2 prosumers following the source-defined Pareto order and never trades noncooperatively. Scope: Proposition 1 for the proposed coalition formation game. The prosumer splits from its previous coalition and merges with the prosumers in V. Scope: Algorithm 1: membership transition into V. Condition: A prosumer belonged to W or V in the previous time slot and now chooses V. The prosumer splits from its previous coalition and merges with the prosumers in W. Scope: Algorithm 1: membership transition into W. Condition: A prosumer belonged to W or V in the previous time slot and now chooses W. All providers in K and receivers in L belong to type 2. Scope: Algorithm 1: final aggregate-energy else branch. Condition: Neither the aggregate excess-supply branch nor the aggregate excess-demand branch applies. The prosumer chooses type 1 of set W. Scope: Algorithm 1: initial membership selection after household energy needs are considered. Condition: The prosumer's residual energy deficiency E_{n,def}(t) is positive. The choice between receiver set L and provider set K is determined by the maximum utility achievable from charging versus discharging. No equal-utility tie rule is supplied. Scope: Choice between state-2 receiver and provider groups. Condition: p_c(t) \u003c p_{n,c}(t) and p_d(t) > p_{n,d}(t).","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Algorithm 1: final aggregate-energy else branch. | Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-demand branch. | Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-supply branch. | Algorithm 1: initial membership selection after household energy needs are considered. | Algorithm 1: membership transition into V. | Algorithm 1: membership transition into W. | Choice between state-2 receiver and provider groups. | Composition of trading coalitions in the proposed scheme. | Individual coalition choice in each time slot of the community P2P market. | Individual coalition selection in each time slot. | Merge operation under Definition 2. | Proposition 1 for the proposed coalition formation game. | Split operation under Definition 3. | Time-slot coalition reconfiguration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E25","v141-b-g01-E025","v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E22","v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g02-E26","v141-b-g02-E38","v141-b-g03-E07"]},"constraint_refs":["31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C009","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C010","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C011","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C012","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C013","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C014","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C015","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C016","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C017"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"b204929429696d3f4ea470fe63e854db6af0186416f1dd74bae21011ed8d3283","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004","node_id":"matching.counterparties","function":"matching.counterparties","unit_id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["31-coalition_formation"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. trading, which is determined by a mid-market rate rule [39], a pro- ha sumer decid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Prosumers that are in state 1 form coalition 1 (Fig. 1) and conduct P2P trading among themselves using the mechanism proposed in [39] via a mid-market pricing scheme. As for prosumers in state 2, each prosumer needs to decide either be a provider or a receiver of energy and thus be in the set $\\mathcal{K}$ or $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively. Once the decision is taken by prosumers, as shown in Fig. 2, each prosumer $n$ who was in state 1 in the previous time slot $t-1$ and would like to be in state 2 at $t$, splits itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who decide to be in state 2 and thus becomes a part of a new coalition. A similar transition can also happen for state 2 players. Finally, P2P trading between the receivers and providers within coalition 2 is conducted using prices derived in (18) and (19).","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Prosumers that are in state 1 form coalition 1 (Fig. 1) and conduct P2P trading among themselves using the mechanism proposed in [39] via a mid-market pricing scheme. As for prosumers in state 2, each prosumer needs to decide either be a provider or a receiver of energy and thus be in the set $\\mathcal{K}$ or $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively. Once the decision is taken by prosumers, as shown in Fig. 2, each prosumer $n$ who was in state 1 in the previous time slot $t-1$ and would like to be in state 2 at $t$, splits itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who decide to be in state 2 and thus becomes a part of a new coalition. A similar transition can also happen for state 2 players. Finally, P2P trading between the receivers and providers within coalition 2 is conducted using prices derived in (18) and (19)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"24: Total available energy from $\\mathcal{K}$ is $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 25: Total demand from $\\mathcal{L}$ is $\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 26: if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)>\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ then 27: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ finally chooses type 2. 28: Calculate excess energy $E_{ex,s}(t)=\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)-\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 29: Set $e_{n,d}(t)=e_{n,d}(t)-\\min(e_{n,d}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,s}(t)}{K}), \\forall n\\in\\mathcal{K}$. 30: if $e_{n,d}(t)>0$ then 31: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in type 2. 32: else 33: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 34: end if","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"24: Total available energy from $\\mathcal{K}$ is $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 25: Total demand from $\\mathcal{L}$ is $\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 26: if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)>\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ then 27: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ finally chooses type 2. 28: Calculate excess energy $E_{ex,s}(t)=\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)-\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 29: Set $e_{n,d}(t)=e_{n,d}(t)-\\min(e_{n,d}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,s}(t)}{K}), \\forall n\\in\\mathcal{K}$. 30: if $e_{n,d}(t)>0$ then 31: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in type 2. 32: else 33: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 34: end if"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"35: else if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\u003c\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ 36: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ finally chooses type 2. 37: Calculate excess demand $E_{ex,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)-\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 38: Set $e_{m,c}(t)=e_{m,c}(t)-\\min(e_{m,c}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,d}(t)}{L}), \\forall m\\in\\mathcal{L}$. 39: if $e_{m,c}(t)>0$ then 40: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$. 41: else 42: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 43: end if","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"35: else if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\u003c\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ 36: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ finally chooses type 2. 37: Calculate excess demand $E_{ex,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)-\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 38: Set $e_{m,c}(t)=e_{m,c}(t)-\\min(e_{m,c}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,d}(t)}{L}), \\forall m\\in\\mathcal{L}$. 39: if $e_{m,c}(t)>0$ then 40: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$. 41: else 42: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 43: end if"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in and belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer n that was in / in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The formation of coalitions between different prosumers for P2P trading is influenced by a prosumer’s decision to socially interact with other prosumers for P2P energy trading. To take the decision, in time slot $t$, a prosumer first meets its demand by energy from its solar panels and storage. Second, it calculates its available surplus, deficiency, and the SoC of the battery. Third, based on the price threshold of the prosumer from (11) and (13) and the available energy prices for P2P trading, which is determined by a mid-market rate rule [39], a prosumer decides whether it wants to charge or discharge its battery to participate in P2P trading. In other words, each prosumer $n$ decides whether it wants to be in state 1 or 2 and utilize the properties of Pareto order to choose the appropriate coalition.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"The formation of coalitions between different prosumers for P2P trading is influenced by a prosumer’s decision to socially interact with other prosumers for P2P energy trading. To take the decision, in time slot $t$, a prosumer first meets its demand by energy from its solar panels and storage. Second, it calculates its available surplus, deficiency, and the SoC of the battery. Third, based on the price threshold of the prosumer from (11) and (13) and the available energy prices for P2P trading, which is determined by a mid-market rate rule [39], a prosumer decides whether it wants to charge or discharge its battery to participate in P2P trading. In other words, each prosumer $n$ decides whether it wants to be in state 1 or 2 and utilize the properties of Pareto order to choose the appropriate coalition."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E025","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Coalition formation framework","anchor_text":"he coalition. Indeed, a prosumer may also choose to take its own strategy without being a part of an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Thus, Pareto order bases the preference on the individual payoffs to the prosumers rather than the coalition value \\nu. Now, based on the Pareto order, each prosumer n decides on the coalition it wants to form in each time slot during a day with the purpose to maximize its utility. To do so, we assume that each prosumer uses merge and split rules for forming coalitions with one another.\n3.2.1. Merge and split rules\nMerge and split rules are two fundamental rules for forming or breaking coalitions based on Pareto order [50]. They can be defined as follows.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Thus, Pareto order bases the preference on the individual payoffs to the prosumers rather than the coalition value \\nu. Now, based on the Pareto order, each prosumer n decides on the coalition it wants to form in each time slot during a day with the purpose to maximize its utility. To do so, we assume that each prosumer uses merge and split rules for forming coalitions with one another.\n3.2.1. Merge and split rules\nMerge and split rules are two fundamental rules for forming or breaking coalitions based on Pareto order [50]. They can be defined as follows."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Merge and split rules","anchor_text":"3.2.1. Merge and split rules Merge and split rules are two fundamental rules for forming or 2) break","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 2. A collection of disjoint coalition $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, $\\mathcal{A}_i\\subset\\mathcal{S}$ can agree to merge into a single coalition $\\mathcal{B}=\\cup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal{A}_n$, if the new coalition $\\mathcal{B}$ is preferred over $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}\\triangleright\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, by the players according to the Pareto order described in Definition 1.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Definition 2. A collection of disjoint coalition $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, $\\mathcal{A}_i\\subset\\mathcal{S}$ can agree to merge into a single coalition $\\mathcal{B}=\\cup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal{A}_n$, if the new coalition $\\mathcal{B}$ is preferred over $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}\\triangleright\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, by the players according to the Pareto order described in Definition 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Merge and split rules","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. This figure shows the social cooperation of prosumers of similar states (within study. coali","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 3. A coalition $\\mathcal{B}=\\cup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal{A}_n, \\mathcal{A}_n\\subset\\mathcal{S}$ can split into smaller coalitions $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$ if the resulting coalitions are preferred by the players over $\\mathcal{B}$, i.e., $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}\\triangleright\\mathcal{B}$, as per the Pareto order in Definition 1.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Definition 3. A coalition $\\mathcal{B}=\\cup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal{A}_n, \\mathcal{A}_n\\subset\\mathcal{S}$ can split into smaller coalitions $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$ if the resulting coalitions are preferred by the players over $\\mathcal{B}$, i.e., $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}\\triangleright\\mathcal{B}$, as per the Pareto order in Definition 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"tion structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/ discharge batteries with who w","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Here, it is important to note that, in the proposed coalition formation structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with who will discharge/charge. This is due to the fact that, as the proposed scheme is designed, the charging and discharging price needs to satisfy the conditions explained in Table 2, where the threshold price $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ include the associated charging and discharging degradation cost of batteries. The motivation behind the choice of such prices is the fact that when a prosumer may want its battery to be charged or discharged for trading in the peer-to-peer market, the trading price should reflect the associated battery degradation cost due to charging and discharging, and at the same time, have enough incentive to make the price beneficial for prosumers to put their batteries into the market. Now, prosumers who participate in peer-to-peer trading market without battery do not need to consider such degradation cost and therefore the trading price does not include the additional item to compensate for the cost of battery degradation [39,51]. Given this context, as the proposed model is designed, these prices will always fall within the case 3 of Table 2, which subsequently removes the possibility of a trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with prosumers who will discharge/charge at the same time.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Here, it is important to note that, in the proposed coalition formation structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with who will discharge/charge. This is due to the fact that, as the proposed scheme is designed, the charging and discharging price needs to satisfy the conditions explained in Table 2, where the threshold price $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ include the associated charging and discharging degradation cost of batteries. The motivation behind the choice of such prices is the fact that when a prosumer may want its battery to be charged or discharged for trading in the peer-to-peer market, the trading price should reflect the associated battery degradation cost due to charging and discharging, and at the same time, have enough incentive to make the price beneficial for prosumers to put their batteries into the market. Now, prosumers who participate in peer-to-peer trading market without battery do not need to consider such degradation cost and therefore the trading price does not include the additional item to compensate for the cost of battery degradation [39,51]. Given this context, as the proposed model is designed, these prices will always fall within the case 3 of Table 2, which subsequently removes the possibility of a trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with prosumers who will discharge/charge at the same time."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"p , p itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who de- no cide to be in state ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"As can be seen from Algorithm 1, the formation of a coalition is dynamic in nature. That is, the coalition formation algorithm runs in every time slot to reconfigure the members of each coalition that would achieve the maximum benefit for the prosumers. The correlation between different time slots is captured through the state of charge of storage devices that varies across different time slots according to its charging and discharging pattern. Of course, it is also possible that the same coalition structure may hold for multiple consecutive time slots if Algorithm 1 identifies that structure is beneficial for the relevant time slots.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"As can be seen from Algorithm 1, the formation of a coalition is dynamic in nature. That is, the coalition formation algorithm runs in every time slot to reconfigure the members of each coalition that would achieve the maximum benefit for the prosumers. The correlation between different time slots is captured through the state of charge of storage devices that varies across different time slots according to its charging and discharging pattern. Of course, it is also possible that the same coalition structure may hold for multiple consecutive time slots if Algorithm 1 identifies that structure is beneficial for the relevant time slots."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r2__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"e, case 1, prosumer n belongs to the coalition of prosumers that are in or state 2. As for case 2, a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Proposition 1. In the proposed \\Gamma, at any time slot t, a prosumer n \\in N always forms a coalition with prosumers that are either in state 1 or state 2 following the Pareto order defined in Definition 1, and never trades non-cooperatively.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Proposition 1. In the proposed \\Gamma, at any time slot t, a prosumer n \\in N always forms a coalition with prosumers that are either in state 1 or state 2 following the Pareto order defined in Definition 1, and never trades non-cooperatively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in and belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer n that was in / in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{V}$. 48: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{W}$. 49: A stable coalition structure for P2P trading is achieved for time slot $t$. 50: end for","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"44: else 45: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{V}$. 48: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{W}$. 49: A stable coalition structure for P2P trading is achieved for time slot $t$. 50: end for"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"s (within a coalition) and their transfer between different coalitions as outlined in this }, 21: Fo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"16: if $E_{n,def}(t)>0$ then 17: Prosumer $n$ chooses to be type 1 of set $\\mathcal{W}$. 18: else 19: Determine $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ following (11) and (13) respectively. 20: Each prosumer follow Table 2 to initially decide whether it wants to be in $\\mathcal{W}$ or $\\mathcal{V}$ prosumer. 21: For type 2, providers and receivers become elements of set $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively, i.e., $\\mathcal{V}=\\mathcal{K}\\cup\\mathcal{L}$. 22: end if 23: end for","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"16: if $E_{n,def}(t)>0$ then 17: Prosumer $n$ chooses to be type 1 of set $\\mathcal{W}$. 18: else 19: Determine $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ following (11) and (13) respectively. 20: Each prosumer follow Table 2 to initially decide whether it wants to be in $\\mathcal{W}$ or $\\mathcal{V}$ prosumer. 21: For type 2, providers and receivers become elements of set $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively, i.e., $\\mathcal{V}=\\mathcal{K}\\cup\\mathcal{L}$. 22: end if 23: end for"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E38","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"= { n : en, c ( t ) > 0and pc ( t ) \u003c pn, c (t ), pd ( t ) pn, d (t )} (20) he and he = {n : en, d (","equation_id":"20-22","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Proof. According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot $t$, prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ and $\\mathcal{K}$ charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where $\\mathcal{L}=\\{n:e_{n,c}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)\\ge p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (20) and $\\mathcal{K}=\\{n:e_{n,d}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\\ge p_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (21). When $p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)$, the decision of a prosumer $n\\in N$ to be in $\\mathcal{L}$ or $\\mathcal{K}$ is determined by the maximum utility that it achieves from charging and discharging. That is, $n\\in\\mathcal{L}$ if $U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}$; $n\\in\\mathcal{K}$ if $U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}$ (22).","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Proof. According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot $t$, prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ and $\\mathcal{K}$ charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where $\\mathcal{L}=\\{n:e_{n,c}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)\\ge p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (20) and $\\mathcal{K}=\\{n:e_{n,d}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\\ge p_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (21). When $p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)$, the decision of a prosumer $n\\in N$ to be in $\\mathcal{L}$ or $\\mathcal{K}$ is determined by the maximum utility that it achieves from charging and discharging. That is, $n\\in\\mathcal{L}$ if $U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}$; $n\\in\\mathcal{K}$ if $U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}$ (22)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E024","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Coalition formation framework","anchor_text":"he coalition. Indeed, a prosumer may also choose to take its own strategy without being a part of an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 1. Consider two collections of coalition A and B of the same set of prosumers N. For a prosumer n \\in N, u_A(n) and u_B(n) denote the utility to prosumer n within coalition A and B respectively. Now, coalition A is preferred by prosumer n, \\forall n \\in N, over the coalition B by Pareto order, indicated by A ▷ B, if u_A(n) \\ge u_B(n), with an equality for at least one player.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Definition 1. Consider two collections of coalition A and B of the same set of prosumers N. For a prosumer n \\in N, u_A(n) and u_B(n) denote the utility to prosumer n within coalition A and B respectively. Now, coalition A is preferred by prosumer n, \\forall n \\in N, over the coalition B by Pareto order, indicated by A ▷ B, if u_A(n) \\ge u_B(n), with an equality for at least one player."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Coalition formation framework","anchor_text":"he coalition. Indeed, a prosumer may also choose to take its own strategy without being a part of an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 1. Consider two collections of coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ of the same set of prosumers $\\mathcal{N}$. For a prosumer $n\\in\\mathcal{N}$, $u_{\\mathcal{A}}(n)$ and $u_{\\mathcal{B}}(n)$ denote the utility to prosumer $n$ within coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ respectively. Now, coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ is preferred by prosumer $n$, $\\forall n\\in\\mathcal{N}$, over the coalition $\\mathcal{B}$ by Pareto order, indicated by $\\mathcal{A}\\triangleright\\mathcal{B}$, if $u_{\\mathcal{A}}(n)\\ge u_{\\mathcal{B}}(n)$, with an equality for at least one player.","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Definition 1. Consider two collections of coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ of the same set of prosumers $\\mathcal{N}$. For a prosumer $n\\in\\mathcal{N}$, $u_{\\mathcal{A}}(n)$ and $u_{\\mathcal{B}}(n)$ denote the utility to prosumer $n$ within coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ respectively. Now, coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ is preferred by prosumer $n$, $\\forall n\\in\\mathcal{N}$, over the coalition $\\mathcal{B}$ by Pareto order, indicated by $\\mathcal{A}\\triangleright\\mathcal{B}$, if $u_{\\mathcal{A}}(n)\\ge u_{\\mathcal{B}}(n)$, with an equality for at least one player."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"= { n : en, c ( t ) > 0and pc ( t ) \u003c pn, c (t ), pd ( t ) pn, d (t )} (20) he and he = {n : en, d (","equation_id":"20-22","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot t, prosumers in \\mathcal{L} and \\mathcal{K} charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where\n\\[\\mathcal{L} = \\{ n : e_{n,c}(t) > 0 \\text{ and } p_c(t) \u003c p_{n,c}(t),\\; p_d(t) \\ge p_{n,d}(t) \\} \\tag{20}\\]\nand\n\\[\\mathcal{K} = \\{n : e_{n,d}(t) > 0 \\text{ and } p_c(t) \\ge p_{n,c}(t),\\; p_d(t) > p_{n,d}(t)\\}. \\tag{21}\\]","paper":"31-coalition_formation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot t, prosumers in \\mathcal{L} and \\mathcal{K} charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where\n\\[\\mathcal{L} = \\{ n : e_{n,c}(t) > 0 \\text{ and } p_c(t) \u003c p_{n,c}(t),\\; p_d(t) \\ge p_{n,d}(t) \\} \\tag{20}\\]\nand\n\\[\\mathcal{K} = \\{n : e_{n,d}(t) > 0 \\text{ and } p_c(t) \\ge p_{n,c}(t),\\; p_d(t) > p_{n,d}(t)\\}. \\tag{21}\\]"}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["coalition_reconfiguration_and_membership_selection"],"projection_statuses":["composite"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L4-bc7ef83e10","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L4","ontological_kind":"coalition_reconfiguration_and_membership_selection"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","paper_id":"31-coalition_formation","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"matching.counterparties","source_occurrences":[{"id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Splitting from the previous coalition and merging with state-2 peers precedes trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Scope: Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. Condition: A prosumer in state 1 at t-1 chooses state 2 at t and changes coalitions. State-1 trading within coalition 1 uses the mid-market pricing scheme referenced in [39]. Scope: P2P trading among members of coalition 1. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 1. Trading within coalition 2 uses the local trading prices derived in (18) and (19). Scope: P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 2. The pricing design removes the possibility of a contemporaneous trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries for trading and prosumers who do. Scope: Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. Condition: Prices without battery-degradation compensation fall within case 3 of Table 2, as specified for the proposed model. State-1 trading assumes zero contribution of battery energy to flows across trading entities. Scope: Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. Condition: The prosumer is in state 1. State-2 trading permits selling energy discharged from a battery to other prosumers and buying peer energy to charge a battery for future needs. Scope: Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 2. The source constrains battery exchange so charging and discharging exceed neither the rated battery rate nor the network energy-transfer limit; the network transfer cap protects recommended node-voltage limits. Scope: State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network. Condition: Battery charging or discharging is undertaken for P2P trading at time t. Total energy charged by receivers must equal total energy discharged by providers. Scope: Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. Condition: State-2 providers and receivers exchange battery energy through P2P trading. The baseline model does not include state-2 sales of battery energy to the CPS. Scope: Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. Condition: A state-2 prosumer would sell battery energy to the CPS. In this proposed extension, a prosumer may sell energy to the CPS through the P2P mechanism. Scope: Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. Condition: The CPS participates in the local market as a peer and allows prosumers to negotiate the per-unit price of energy sold to it.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. | Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. | Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. | Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. | Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. | P2P trading among members of coalition 1. | P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. | Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. | Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. | State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24","v141-b-g01-E009","v141-b-g01-E010","v141-b-g01-E013","v141-b-g01-E014","v141-b-g02-E26","v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E34"]},"constraint_refs":["31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","source_rule_sha256":"56f4c146060c93c1fc0141a3c86155258f96faaa3a2fec56e3882d5d888fdf77"},{"id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::V::matching.counterparties","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"Local quantity allocation feeds final coalition grouping: all receivers choose type 2; providers choose type 2 when their adjusted discharge quantity is positive and W otherwise. Scope: Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-supply branch. Condition: Total provider discharge exceeds total requested receiver charging energy. Local quantity allocation feeds final coalition grouping: all providers choose type 2, while the adjusted receiver charging quantity is tested for positivity to select V rather than W membership. The source's participant-index ambiguity remains unresolved. Scope: Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-demand branch. Condition: Total requested receiver charging energy exceeds total provider discharge. Available P2P trading prices enter the prosumer's decision about state 1 versus state 2 and the corresponding trading coalition, together with individual price thresholds and Pareto comparison. Scope: Individual coalition choice in each time slot of the community P2P market. Each prosumer selects its coalition under the source-defined Pareto order with the objective of maximizing its individual utility, using merge-and-split rules. Scope: Individual coalition selection in each time slot. A collection of disjoint coalitions may agree to merge into its union coalition. Scope: Merge operation under Definition 2. Condition: The proposed union coalition is preferred by the players over the existing collection according to the source-defined Pareto order. A coalition may split into the preferred collection of smaller coalitions. Scope: Split operation under Definition 3. Condition: The resulting smaller coalitions are preferred by the players over the original coalition according to the source-defined Pareto order. A coalition cannot mix prosumers who do not charge or discharge batteries for trading with prosumers who charge or discharge batteries for trading. Scope: Composition of trading coalitions in the proposed scheme. The coalition formation algorithm runs in every time slot to reconfigure membership for maximum prosumer benefit. Scope: Time-slot coalition reconfiguration. At each time slot, each prosumer forms a coalition with state-1 or state-2 prosumers following the source-defined Pareto order and never trades noncooperatively. Scope: Proposition 1 for the proposed coalition formation game. The prosumer splits from its previous coalition and merges with the prosumers in V. Scope: Algorithm 1: membership transition into V. Condition: A prosumer belonged to W or V in the previous time slot and now chooses V. The prosumer splits from its previous coalition and merges with the prosumers in W. Scope: Algorithm 1: membership transition into W. Condition: A prosumer belonged to W or V in the previous time slot and now chooses W. All providers in K and receivers in L belong to type 2. Scope: Algorithm 1: final aggregate-energy else branch. Condition: Neither the aggregate excess-supply branch nor the aggregate excess-demand branch applies. The prosumer chooses type 1 of set W. Scope: Algorithm 1: initial membership selection after household energy needs are considered. Condition: The prosumer's residual energy deficiency E_{n,def}(t) is positive. The choice between receiver set L and provider set K is determined by the maximum utility achievable from charging versus discharging. No equal-utility tie rule is supplied. Scope: Choice between state-2 receiver and provider groups. Condition: p_c(t) \u003c p_{n,c}(t) and p_d(t) > p_{n,d}(t).","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Algorithm 1: final aggregate-energy else branch. | Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-demand branch. | Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-supply branch. | Algorithm 1: initial membership selection after household energy needs are considered. | Algorithm 1: membership transition into V. | Algorithm 1: membership transition into W. | Choice between state-2 receiver and provider groups. | Composition of trading coalitions in the proposed scheme. | Individual coalition choice in each time slot of the community P2P market. | Individual coalition selection in each time slot. | Merge operation under Definition 2. | Proposition 1 for the proposed coalition formation game. | Split operation under Definition 3. | Time-slot coalition reconfiguration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E25","v141-b-g01-E025","v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E22","v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g02-E26","v141-b-g02-E38","v141-b-g03-E07"]},"constraint_refs":["31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C009","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C010","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C011","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C012","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C013","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C014","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C015","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C016","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C017"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"b204929429696d3f4ea470fe63e854db6af0186416f1dd74bae21011ed8d3283"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Splitting from the previous coalition and merging with state-2 peers precedes trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Scope: Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. Condition: A prosumer in state 1 at t-1 chooses state 2 at t and changes coalitions.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. trading, which is determined by a mid-market rate rule [39], a pro- ha sumer decid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Prosumers that are in state 1 form coalition 1 (Fig. 1) and conduct P2P trading among themselves using the mechanism proposed in [39] via a mid-market pricing scheme. As for prosumers in state 2, each prosumer needs to decide either be a provider or a receiver of energy and thus be in the set $\\mathcal{K}$ or $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively. Once the decision is taken by prosumers, as shown in Fig. 2, each prosumer $n$ who was in state 1 in the previous time slot $t-1$ and would like to be in state 2 at $t$, splits itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who decide to be in state 2 and thus becomes a part of a new coalition. A similar transition can also happen for state 2 players. Finally, P2P trading between the receivers and providers within coalition 2 is conducted using prices derived in (18) and (19)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local quantity allocation feeds final coalition grouping: all receivers choose type 2; providers choose type 2 when their adjusted discharge quantity is positive and W otherwise. Scope: Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-supply branch. Condition: Total provider discharge exceeds total requested receiver charging energy.","expression":"\\begin{aligned} &E_{ex,s}(t)=\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)-\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t),\\\\ &\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)>\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t) \\Rightarrow \\Bigl[\\mathcal L\\subseteq\\mathcal V \\;\\land\\; \\forall n\\in\\mathcal K,\\; e'_{n,d}(t)=e_{n,d}(t)-\\min\\!\\bigl(e_{n,d}(t),E_{ex,s}(t)/K\\bigr) \\;\\land\\; \\bigl(n\\in\\mathcal V \\Longleftrightarrow e'_{n,d}(t)>0\\bigr) \\;\\land\\; \\bigl(n\\in\\mathcal W \\Longleftrightarrow e'_{n,d}(t)\\le 0\\bigr)\\Bigr]. \\end{aligned}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["x_e_nd","x_e_mc"],"parameter_refs":["theta_K","theta_L"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"24: Total available energy from $\\mathcal{K}$ is $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 25: Total demand from $\\mathcal{L}$ is $\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 26: if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)>\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ then 27: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ finally chooses type 2. 28: Calculate excess energy $E_{ex,s}(t)=\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)-\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 29: Set $e_{n,d}(t)=e_{n,d}(t)-\\min(e_{n,d}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,s}(t)}{K}), \\forall n\\in\\mathcal{K}$. 30: if $e_{n,d}(t)>0$ then 31: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in type 2. 32: else 33: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 34: end if"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local quantity allocation feeds final coalition grouping: all providers choose type 2, while the adjusted receiver charging quantity is tested for positivity to select V rather than W membership. The source's participant-index ambiguity remains unresolved. Scope: Algorithm 1: final coalition membership in the excess-demand branch. Condition: Total requested receiver charging energy exceeds total provider discharge.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E21"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"35: else if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\u003c\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ 36: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ finally chooses type 2. 37: Calculate excess demand $E_{ex,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)-\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 38: Set $e_{m,c}(t)=e_{m,c}(t)-\\min(e_{m,c}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,d}(t)}{L}), \\forall m\\in\\mathcal{L}$. 39: if $e_{m,c}(t)>0$ then 40: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$. 41: else 42: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 43: end if"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Available P2P trading prices enter the prosumer's decision about state 1 versus state 2 and the corresponding trading coalition, together with individual price thresholds and Pareto comparison. Scope: Individual coalition choice in each time slot of the community P2P market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in and belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer n that was in / in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The formation of coalitions between different prosumers for P2P trading is influenced by a prosumer’s decision to socially interact with other prosumers for P2P energy trading. To take the decision, in time slot $t$, a prosumer first meets its demand by energy from its solar panels and storage. Second, it calculates its available surplus, deficiency, and the SoC of the battery. Third, based on the price threshold of the prosumer from (11) and (13) and the available energy prices for P2P trading, which is determined by a mid-market rate rule [39], a prosumer decides whether it wants to charge or discharge its battery to participate in P2P trading. In other words, each prosumer $n$ decides whether it wants to be in state 1 or 2 and utilize the properties of Pareto order to choose the appropriate coalition."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each prosumer selects its coalition under the source-defined Pareto order with the objective of maximizing its individual utility, using merge-and-split rules. Scope: Individual coalition selection in each time slot.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E025"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E025","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Coalition formation framework","anchor_text":"he coalition. Indeed, a prosumer may also choose to take its own strategy without being a part of an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Thus, Pareto order bases the preference on the individual payoffs to the prosumers rather than the coalition value \\nu. Now, based on the Pareto order, each prosumer n decides on the coalition it wants to form in each time slot during a day with the purpose to maximize its utility. To do so, we assume that each prosumer uses merge and split rules for forming coalitions with one another.\n3.2.1. Merge and split rules\nMerge and split rules are two fundamental rules for forming or breaking coalitions based on Pareto order [50]. They can be defined as follows."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A collection of disjoint coalitions may agree to merge into its union coalition. Scope: Merge operation under Definition 2. Condition: The proposed union coalition is preferred by the players over the existing collection according to the source-defined Pareto order.","expression":"\\left(\\{\\mathcal A_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal A_i\\}\\text{ disjoint},\\ \\mathcal A_i\\subset\\mathcal S\\right)\\Rightarrow\\left(\\text{merge to }\\mathcal B=\\bigcup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal A_n\\text{ is allowed iff }\\mathcal B\\triangleright\\{\\mathcal A_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal A_i\\}\\right).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Merge and split rules","anchor_text":"3.2.1. Merge and split rules Merge and split rules are two fundamental rules for forming or 2) break","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 2. A collection of disjoint coalition $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, $\\mathcal{A}_i\\subset\\mathcal{S}$ can agree to merge into a single coalition $\\mathcal{B}=\\cup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal{A}_n$, if the new coalition $\\mathcal{B}$ is preferred over $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}\\triangleright\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$, by the players according to the Pareto order described in Definition 1."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A coalition may split into the preferred collection of smaller coalitions. Scope: Split operation under Definition 3. Condition: The resulting smaller coalitions are preferred by the players over the original coalition according to the source-defined Pareto order.","expression":"\\left(\\mathcal B=\\bigcup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal A_n,\\ \\mathcal A_n\\subset\\mathcal S\\right)\\Rightarrow\\left(\\text{split into }\\{\\mathcal A_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal A_i\\}\\text{ is allowed iff }\\{\\mathcal A_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal A_i\\}\\triangleright\\mathcal B\\right).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E16"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Merge and split rules","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. This figure shows the social cooperation of prosumers of similar states (within study. coali","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 3. A coalition $\\mathcal{B}=\\cup_{n=1}^{i}\\mathcal{A}_n, \\mathcal{A}_n\\subset\\mathcal{S}$ can split into smaller coalitions $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}$ if the resulting coalitions are preferred by the players over $\\mathcal{B}$, i.e., $\\{\\mathcal{A}_1,\\mathcal{A}_2,\\ldots,\\mathcal{A}_i\\}\\triangleright\\mathcal{B}$, as per the Pareto order in Definition 1."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C007","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A coalition cannot mix prosumers who do not charge or discharge batteries for trading with prosumers who charge or discharge batteries for trading. Scope: Composition of trading coalitions in the proposed scheme.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E26"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"tion structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/ discharge batteries with who w","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Here, it is important to note that, in the proposed coalition formation structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with who will discharge/charge. This is due to the fact that, as the proposed scheme is designed, the charging and discharging price needs to satisfy the conditions explained in Table 2, where the threshold price $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ include the associated charging and discharging degradation cost of batteries. The motivation behind the choice of such prices is the fact that when a prosumer may want its battery to be charged or discharged for trading in the peer-to-peer market, the trading price should reflect the associated battery degradation cost due to charging and discharging, and at the same time, have enough incentive to make the price beneficial for prosumers to put their batteries into the market. Now, prosumers who participate in peer-to-peer trading market without battery do not need to consider such degradation cost and therefore the trading price does not include the additional item to compensate for the cost of battery degradation [39,51]. Given this context, as the proposed model is designed, these prices will always fall within the case 3 of Table 2, which subsequently removes the possibility of a trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with prosumers who will discharge/charge at the same time."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The coalition formation algorithm runs in every time slot to reconfigure membership for maximum prosumer benefit. Scope: Time-slot coalition reconfiguration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E25"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"p , p itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who de- no cide to be in state ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"As can be seen from Algorithm 1, the formation of a coalition is dynamic in nature. That is, the coalition formation algorithm runs in every time slot to reconfigure the members of each coalition that would achieve the maximum benefit for the prosumers. The correlation between different time slots is captured through the state of charge of storage devices that varies across different time slots according to its charging and discharging pattern. Of course, it is also possible that the same coalition structure may hold for multiple consecutive time slots if Algorithm 1 identifies that structure is beneficial for the relevant time slots."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C009","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"At each time slot, each prosumer forms a coalition with state-1 or state-2 prosumers following the source-defined Pareto order and never trades noncooperatively. Scope: Proposition 1 for the proposed coalition formation game.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E07"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C009","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r2__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"e, case 1, prosumer n belongs to the coalition of prosumers that are in or state 2. As for case 2, a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Proposition 1. In the proposed \\Gamma, at any time slot t, a prosumer n \\in N always forms a coalition with prosumers that are either in state 1 or state 2 following the Pareto order defined in Definition 1, and never trades non-cooperatively."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C010","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The prosumer splits from its previous coalition and merges with the prosumers in V. Scope: Algorithm 1: membership transition into V. Condition: A prosumer belonged to W or V in the previous time slot and now chooses V.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E22"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C010","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in and belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer n that was in / in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{V}$. 48: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{W}$. 49: A stable coalition structure for P2P trading is achieved for time slot $t$. 50: end for"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C011","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The prosumer splits from its previous coalition and merges with the prosumers in W. Scope: Algorithm 1: membership transition into W. Condition: A prosumer belonged to W or V in the previous time slot and now chooses W.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E22"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C011","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in and belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer n that was in / in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{V}$. 48: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{W}$. 49: A stable coalition structure for P2P trading is achieved for time slot $t$. 50: end for"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C012","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"All providers in K and receivers in L belong to type 2. Scope: Algorithm 1: final aggregate-energy else branch. Condition: Neither the aggregate excess-supply branch nor the aggregate excess-demand branch applies.","expression":"\\left(\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)\\right)\\Rightarrow\\left(\\mathcal K\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\ \\land\\ \\mathcal L\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\right).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["x_e_nd","x_e_mc"],"parameter_refs":["theta_K","theta_L"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E22"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C012","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"24: Total available energy from $\\mathcal{K}$ is $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 25: Total demand from $\\mathcal{L}$ is $\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 26: if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)>\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ then 27: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ finally chooses type 2. 28: Calculate excess energy $E_{ex,s}(t)=\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)-\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$. 29: Set $e_{n,d}(t)=e_{n,d}(t)-\\min(e_{n,d}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,s}(t)}{K}), \\forall n\\in\\mathcal{K}$. 30: if $e_{n,d}(t)>0$ then 31: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in type 2. 32: else 33: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 34: end if"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"35: else if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\u003c\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ 36: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ finally chooses type 2. 37: Calculate excess demand $E_{ex,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)-\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 38: Set $e_{m,c}(t)=e_{m,c}(t)-\\min(e_{m,c}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,d}(t)}{L}), \\forall m\\in\\mathcal{L}$. 39: if $e_{m,c}(t)>0$ then 40: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$. 41: else 42: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 43: end if"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in and belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer n that was in / in ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"44: else 45: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ belong to type 2. 46: end if 47: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{V}$. 48: Each prosumer $n$ that was in $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ in the previous time slot and decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$, splits from $\\mathcal{W}/\\mathcal{V}$ and merges to the prosumers in $\\mathcal{W}$. 49: A stable coalition structure for P2P trading is achieved for time slot $t$. 50: end for"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C013","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The prosumer chooses type 1 of set W. Scope: Algorithm 1: initial membership selection after household energy needs are considered. Condition: The prosumer's residual energy deficiency E_{n,def}(t) is positive.","expression":"E_{n,def}(t)>0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ n\\in\\mathcal W(t)\\quad\\text{(type 1)}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["x_E_def"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C013","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"s (within a coalition) and their transfer between different coalitions as outlined in this }, 21: Fo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"16: if $E_{n,def}(t)>0$ then 17: Prosumer $n$ chooses to be type 1 of set $\\mathcal{W}$. 18: else 19: Determine $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ following (11) and (13) respectively. 20: Each prosumer follow Table 2 to initially decide whether it wants to be in $\\mathcal{W}$ or $\\mathcal{V}$ prosumer. 21: For type 2, providers and receivers become elements of set $\\mathcal{K}$ and $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively, i.e., $\\mathcal{V}=\\mathcal{K}\\cup\\mathcal{L}$. 22: end if 23: end for"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C014","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The choice between receiver set L and provider set K is determined by the maximum utility achievable from charging versus discharging. No equal-utility tie rule is supplied. Scope: Choice between state-2 receiver and provider groups. Condition: p_c(t) \u003c p_{n,c}(t) and p_d(t) > p_{n,d}(t).","expression":"\\bigl(p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t)\\ \\land\\ p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)\\bigr)\\Rightarrow\\left[\\left(n\\in\\mathcal L \\Longleftrightarrow U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}\\right)\\ \\land\\ \\left(n\\in\\mathcal K \\Longleftrightarrow U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}\\right)\\right].","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["x_e_nc","x_e_nd"],"parameter_refs":["theta_pc","theta_pd","theta_pnc","theta_pnd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E38"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C014","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E38","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"= { n : en, c ( t ) > 0and pc ( t ) \u003c pn, c (t ), pd ( t ) pn, d (t )} (20) he and he = {n : en, d (","equation_id":"20-22","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Proof. According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot $t$, prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ and $\\mathcal{K}$ charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where $\\mathcal{L}=\\{n:e_{n,c}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)\\ge p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (20) and $\\mathcal{K}=\\{n:e_{n,d}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\\ge p_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (21). When $p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)$, the decision of a prosumer $n\\in N$ to be in $\\mathcal{L}$ or $\\mathcal{K}$ is determined by the maximum utility that it achieves from charging and discharging. That is, $n\\in\\mathcal{L}$ if $U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}$; $n\\in\\mathcal{K}$ if $U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}$ (22)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C015","kind":"logical","role":"supporting","statement":"Definition 1: For two coalition collections A and B of the same prosumer set N, A is Pareto-preferred to B, denoted A ▷ B, if every prosumer's utility under A is at least its utility under B, with an equality for at least one player (as printed).","expression":"\\mathcal A\\triangleright\\mathcal B\\iff\\left(\\forall n\\in\\mathcal N:\\ u_{\\mathcal A}(n)\\ge u_{\\mathcal B}(n)\\right)\\ \\land\\ \\left(\\exists n\\in\\mathcal N:\\ u_{\\mathcal A}(n)=u_{\\mathcal B}(n)\\right).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E024","v141-b-g02-E14"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C015","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E024","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Coalition formation framework","anchor_text":"he coalition. Indeed, a prosumer may also choose to take its own strategy without being a part of an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 1. Consider two collections of coalition A and B of the same set of prosumers N. For a prosumer n \\in N, u_A(n) and u_B(n) denote the utility to prosumer n within coalition A and B respectively. Now, coalition A is preferred by prosumer n, \\forall n \\in N, over the coalition B by Pareto order, indicated by A ▷ B, if u_A(n) \\ge u_B(n), with an equality for at least one player."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2 Coalition formation framework","anchor_text":"he coalition. Indeed, a prosumer may also choose to take its own strategy without being a part of an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Definition 1. Consider two collections of coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ of the same set of prosumers $\\mathcal{N}$. For a prosumer $n\\in\\mathcal{N}$, $u_{\\mathcal{A}}(n)$ and $u_{\\mathcal{B}}(n)$ denote the utility to prosumer $n$ within coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ respectively. Now, coalition $\\mathcal{A}$ is preferred by prosumer $n$, $\\forall n\\in\\mathcal{N}$, over the coalition $\\mathcal{B}$ by Pareto order, indicated by $\\mathcal{A}\\triangleright\\mathcal{B}$, if $u_{\\mathcal{A}}(n)\\ge u_{\\mathcal{B}}(n)$, with an equality for at least one player."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C016","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"In the state-2 coalition, the receiver set L and provider set K are defined by positive charging/discharging quantities and threshold-price comparisons according to equations (20)-(21).","expression":"\\mathcal L=\\{n:e_{n,c}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t),\\ p_d(t)\\ge p_{n,d}(t)\\};\\qquad \\mathcal K=\\{n:e_{n,d}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\\ge p_{n,c}(t),\\ p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_e_nc","x_e_nd"],"parameter_refs":["theta_pc","theta_pd","theta_pnc","theta_pnd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E38","v141-b-g03-E09"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C016","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E38","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"= { n : en, c ( t ) > 0and pc ( t ) \u003c pn, c (t ), pd ( t ) pn, d (t )} (20) he and he = {n : en, d (","equation_id":"20-22","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Proof. According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot $t$, prosumers in $\\mathcal{L}$ and $\\mathcal{K}$ charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where $\\mathcal{L}=\\{n:e_{n,c}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)\\ge p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (20) and $\\mathcal{K}=\\{n:e_{n,d}(t)>0\\ \\text{and}\\ p_c(t)\\ge p_{n,c}(t), p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)\\}$ (21). When $p_c(t)\u003cp_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_d(t)>p_{n,d}(t)$, the decision of a prosumer $n\\in N$ to be in $\\mathcal{L}$ or $\\mathcal{K}$ is determined by the maximum utility that it achieves from charging and discharging. That is, $n\\in\\mathcal{L}$ if $U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}$; $n\\in\\mathcal{K}$ if $U_{n,d}(t)|_{e_{n,d}(t)\\ge0}>U_{n,c}(t)|_{e_{n,c}(t)\\ge0}$ (22)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Stability and optimality","anchor_text":"= { n : en, c ( t ) > 0and pc ( t ) \u003c pn, c (t ), pd ( t ) pn, d (t )} (20) he and he = {n : en, d (","equation_id":"20-22","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"According to Algorithm 1, we note that, at any time slot t, prosumers in \\mathcal{L} and \\mathcal{K} charge and discharge their batteries for P2P trading respectively, where\n\\[\\mathcal{L} = \\{ n : e_{n,c}(t) > 0 \\text{ and } p_c(t) \u003c p_{n,c}(t),\\; p_d(t) \\ge p_{n,d}(t) \\} \\tag{20}\\]\nand\n\\[\\mathcal{K} = \\{n : e_{n,d}(t) > 0 \\text{ and } p_c(t) \\ge p_{n,c}(t),\\; p_d(t) > p_{n,d}(t)\\}. \\tag{21}\\]"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C017","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"In the excess-demand branch of Algorithm 1, excess demand is total receiver charging energy minus total provider discharge energy, and each receiver's charging quantity is reduced by the smaller of its charging quantity and E_{ex,d}(t)/L. The subsequent membership test in the source is printed for 'Prosumer n' and is not resolved here.","expression":"\\left(\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\u003c\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)\\right)\\Rightarrow\\left[E_{ex,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)-\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\\ \\land\\ \\forall m\\in\\mathcal L,\\ e'_{m,c}(t)=e_{m,c}(t)-\\min\\!\\bigl(e_{m,c}(t),E_{ex,d}(t)/L\\bigr)\\right].","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["x_e_nd","x_e_mc"],"parameter_refs":["theta_K","theta_L"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E21"],"ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C017","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"in 25: Total demand from is mL = 1 em, c t( ) . 26: if nK= 1 en, d (t ) > mL = 1 em, c (t ) then he ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"35: else if $\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)\u003c\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)$ 36: All prosumers in $\\mathcal{K}$ finally chooses type 2. 37: Calculate excess demand $E_{ex,d}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{L}e_{m,c}(t)-\\sum_{n=1}^{K}e_{n,d}(t)$. 38: Set $e_{m,c}(t)=e_{m,c}(t)-\\min(e_{m,c}(t),\\frac{E_{ex,d}(t)}{L}), \\forall m\\in\\mathcal{L}$. 39: if $e_{m,c}(t)>0$ then 40: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{V}$. 41: else 42: Prosumer $n$ decides to be in $\\mathcal{W}$. 43: end if"}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"x_e_nd","symbol":"e_{n,d}(t)","meaning":"Energy discharged by provider n's battery for state-2 P2P trading at time t, including the adjusted discharge quantity used in Algorithm 1 membership tests.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E38"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::x::x_e_nd"},{"variable_id":"x_e_mc","symbol":"e_{m,c}(t)","meaning":"Energy charged by receiver m's battery for state-2 P2P trading at time t, including the adjusted charging quantity used in Algorithm 1 excess-demand adjustment.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}","unit":"kWh","indices":["m","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::x::x_e_mc"},{"variable_id":"x_e_nc","symbol":"e_{n,c}(t)","meaning":"Energy charged by prosumer n's battery at time t, as used in the state-2 receiver membership condition (20).","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E38"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::x::x_e_nc"},{"variable_id":"x_E_def","symbol":"E_{n,def}(t)","meaning":"Residual energy deficiency of prosumer n at time t, tested before initial type-1 membership selection.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathbb{R}","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::x::x_E_def"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta_pc","symbol":"p_c(t)","meaning":"P2P charging price per unit of energy at time t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g02-E38"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::theta::theta_pc"},{"parameter_id":"theta_pd","symbol":"p_d(t)","meaning":"P2P discharging price per unit of energy at time t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g02-E38"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::theta::theta_pd"},{"parameter_id":"theta_pnc","symbol":"p_{n,c}(t)","meaning":"Threshold charging price of prosumer n at time t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E38"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::theta::theta_pnc"},{"parameter_id":"theta_pnd","symbol":"p_{n,d}(t)","meaning":"Threshold discharging price of prosumer n at time t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E38"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::theta::theta_pnd"},{"parameter_id":"theta_K","symbol":"K","meaning":"Total number of providers in the current state-2 provider set used in Algorithm 1 aggregate-energy expressions.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"count","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::theta::theta_K"},{"parameter_id":"theta_L","symbol":"L","meaning":"Total number of receivers in the current state-2 receiver set used in Algorithm 1 aggregate-energy expressions.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"count","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::theta::theta_L"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-a-M01::matching.counterparties","stage":"L4","subtype":"coalition_reconfiguration_and_membership_selection","projection_status":"composite","target_definition":"Includes two kinds of organizational definition: coalition merging and splitting are executed according to the source-defined Pareto order on individual payoffs; members of W/V and of provider K and receiver L are determined according to state, price thresholds, utility comparison, and adjusted transaction quantity. Trading groups are reorganized on this basis in each time slot.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: the set of prosumer coalitions and their trading-role groups","Core operation one: preference-permitted coalition merging, splitting, and migration across time slots","Core operation two: member selection driven by state and transaction conditions","Output: coalition structure and the members of each group, with process rules and member predicates retained separately"],"variation_fields":["The source Pareto condition is that every member has u_A(n)≥u_B(n), and at least one member takes equality; it is not changed to at least one person strictly improving","State 1 and state 2 are not mixed; W is initially selected when the residual electricity deficit satisfies E_{n,def}(t)>0","When supply is in surplus, the receiver enters V, and the provider selects V/W according to whether the adjusted discharge quantity is positive","The demand-surplus branch judges the received quantity, yet it is printed as Prosumer n; the participant-index ambiguity is unresolved","The L condition of Equation (20) contains p_d(t)≥p_{n,d}(t) and is retained separately from the charging branch of Table 2","When the bidirectional incentive holds, the maximum utilities of charging and discharging are compared; when they are equal, no selection rule is given"],"constraint_refs":["31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C013","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C014","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C015","31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C016"],"paper_id":"31-coalition_formation","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"matching.counterparties","template_code":"CBA"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/270"});
